{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Live trajectory for this metric, measured tonight, relevant to the arithmetic question this ticket raises.\n\n`docker_unaccounted_bytes` across the operator-authorised `systemctl restart docker` at 05:23 UTC:\n\n    2.84 GB   before the restart          (alarm firing)\n    1.42 GB   immediately after           (alarm CLEARED -- real orphan sweep)\n    1.89 GB   steady for ~45 min\n    2.81 GB   at 00:58 PDT                (alarm firing again)\n\nSo the restart genuinely reclaimed ~1.4 GB, and the figure then climbed back over the 2 GiB threshold within about an hour and a half.\n\n## Why this bears on THIS ticket rather than being a separate finding\n\nThis ticket's claim is that the metric `subtracts apparent-size ledger rows from an allocated-size total` — i.e. that the number mixes two different notions of size (`du -sx` allocated blocks versus `docker system df` apparent sizes). If that is right, then part of the 2.81 GB is an ARTEFACT of the arithmetic rather than genuine unreachable residue, and the growth rate above is not a clean measurement of anything.\n\nI am deliberately NOT drawing the obvious conclusion. Two readings fit the same numbers:\n\n- **(a)** orphaned overlay2 really is re-accumulating fast (~0.9 GB in one 15-minute bucket), which would make the restart a temporary reprieve and point at whatever generator is producing it; or\n- **(b)** the arithmetic overstates, and the post-restart 1.42 GB / 1.89 GB baseline is closer to a floor of measurement error than to real residue.\n\nDistinguishing them needs the ground truth this ticket is about: `du -sx --block-size=1` over `/var/lib/docker/overlay2` compared against the ledger sum, on the box, at one instant. Until that is done, the number should not be used to size a remediation.\n\nOne caveat on the step from 1.89 to 2.81 GB: a ~0.9 GB jump inside a single 15-minute bucket is more consistent with a discrete event (an image pull, a build, a container write) than with steady accrual. Worth identifying before assuming a leak.\n\nRecording the series so whoever fixes the arithmetic has a real trace to validate against — a corrected metric should be able to explain these six points, not just produce a different number."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788596124591264001,"uuid":"c497d99b-20ef-4f25-9314-3ddcc353aad3"}